ent uh in  building a team and so you know when I  was at Oklahoma State when I was at  University of Miami I was my own  Personnel director and and I personally  uh evaluated the players before we ever  signed them or drafted them Etc and so I  think Personnel was as much as anything  else in building a team okay but how do  you do that I mean it's all in the eye  of the beholder but you were seeing  something that was different than a lot  of other coaches and GMS were  well you know I emphasize certain things  you know number one was Intelligence I  mean you look at the first four picks  you know uh when I went to the Cowboys  Troy Aikman academic All-American you  know Daryl Johnston academic  All-American Mark stabnoski academic  All-American Tony Tolbert academic  All-American you know that's in the  first four rounds and you know those  players get better and better you don't  have the problems off the field uh they  improve as time goes on and they really  contribute I always said you know I look  for five things I said number one is  intelligence number two I wanted a gym  rat I wanted somebody that was going to  compete you know somebody making a  difference it was shooting pool or pick  up basketball they wanted to compete and  the other thing is I wanted a worker  that uh had passion for what they were  going to do that would willing to work  around the clock or see Beaufort for  instance uh general manager of the Spurs  and he's been down here twice  and he said you know Pop's going to  retire when he says would you be  concerned about a college coach you know  what do you look for in hiring somebody  I said it's no different than what I  talk to companies about you know and  hiring workers and you know hiring  people for any company I said give me  somebody that's intelligent  give me somebody that's got passion for  the job that really wants to put  themselves totally into it and give me  somebody that's willing to work around  the clock to get the job done I don't  care what they know if they don't know  anything about the job they'll learn  what to do and they'll know they'll  learn how to do it so give me those  three things and you're going to have  somebody that's going to be successful  why write a book now  well you first of all you know with all  the people coming down  coming down by attorney Nick Chris and  say hey you got  to read  after I got it  we started not only talking about the  talent and building the team we started  talking about the struggles you know  that I went through and my family went  through you know what yeah both of my  sons played football I never saw either  one of them play a game and you know it  the book was almost an apology to my  sons and my family that hey I would have  liked to have been there before you but  I was always trying to win a  championship I always felt like I had to  outwork the opposing coach and so in  some ways it was apology to my family  and and I talked to my boys about it and  they said hey daddy says we understand  we would have loved to have had you  there uh but by the same token we  benefited from all those you know  successes that you had not the one son  Chad in fact there's a chapter in the  book about it you know he suffered  through alcoholism for many years and uh  he went through recovery and started a  rehab center in St Petersburg tranquil  Shores now he's got a second one right  outside of Austin tranquil Hills super  successful and it's really really  rewarding for me to put that in the book  but how did how much did you beat  yourself self up over that you know do  you take responsibility ability in some  way that your son became an alcoholic  I I don't know that you know  my wife Rhonda  Ron herself and  and too much responsibility for that  but the only thing I say Dan is if I'd  have been there maybe I could have  nipped it in the bud  you know probably the most emotional I  think I've ever been they have a client  reunion uh they're at tranquil Shores  and I've been there three or four times  and they have the mothers and daddies of  the clients that are in recovery go up  and speak at the podium and mothers and  days will get up and I'll be sitting  next to Chad there in the audience and  they'll say Chad  thanks for saving my son's life  one lady got up and said Chad you picked  up my daughter at two o'clock in the  morning drove her around for four hours  talking to her counseling with her and  then Tucker to detox thanks for saving  my daughter's life I mean I'm sitting  here teared up you know and sitting next  to Chad and then I've got to talk I've  got to finish it up and I said listen  you know I won national championships  you know I won Super Bowls and I said  nothing that I've ever done can compare  to what y'all are doing saving lives  well you look at the I know I've talked  about this on on my show about being at  SportsCenter I was working second shift  for 15 years and missed out on an awful  lot and and you never my wife said stop  beating yourself up but it's hard to say  that and you know your kids I have four  kids but you still you hold it in and  you're like God I was I selfish and you  sort of run you know try to find the  balance there and I don't know if  there's a balance in in what we do or  coaching and other professions yeah you  know I speak to companies all the time  and and you know I visit with the CEOs  and the presidents and Etc et cetera  and the thing that I found out people  that are really successful you know  ahead of these companies they don't put  in just 40 hours you know they sacrifice  yeah they have their struggles just like  so it's not just in sports it's not just  hit football coaches I think sometimes  head football coaches take it a little  bit to the extreme sleeping in the  office and stuff but but really anybody  really successful they got to put their  whole body into it it can't be just a  40-hour week he's Jimmy Johnson the Hall  of Famer and the new book is Swagger  Super Bowls brass balls and footballs a  memoir you can get it online wherever  books are sold  I've spoken about this many many times  on the show that not everybody who's a  great coordinator should be a head coach  the difference between being a  coordinator and a head coach on a  day-to-day basis is what  Ron dable called and we talked for about  an hour uh and he asked he said you know  what's the best advice you can give me  I said Brian I said you know you've been  a great coordinator X's and O's and you  know schemes  I said but when you had walked down that  Hall  nobody cared you know if I gave a damn I  said but now  you're the head coach you're the leader  you walk down that Hall and every head  will turn  if you're going to get the best out of  the organization you've got to have  interaction because let me tell you what  no one wants to be ignored by the leader  and I said you've got to have  interaction with the secretary with  Administrative Assistant with their  backup offensive guard with the intern  coach it doesn't always have to be  upbeat and possible but I have some  interaction you know make sure  everybody's on the same page and I was  so impressed with Brian one of the  things he said oh yeah he said in fact I  took Pizza down to the equipment guys  just the other day and I said yeah get  them all on the same page  but you put in more time college or uh  NFL as far as a head coach  Brad y'all asked me on the show one time  and said you know how about the  differences too I said Terry  there's not a world of difference  between college coaching and pro  coaching there's a galaxy of difference  it is I mean the length of the scene  when you're the head coach of a college  you know especially one of those top 12  schools you're a father figure you're a  mentor you know you've got young kids  that left home for the first time in  their life they got girlfriend problems  so you're in your office counseling them  you know now in professional football  hey it's you know the parody it is so  close between the best and the worst if  you don't go out there and play well  you're gonna get beat  but that doesn't happen in the top  colleges they have so much more Talent  than the teams they're playing you know  my wife Rhonda you could have won 10  games at University of Miami you know  but but if you don't play well in pro  football you're going to get beat  when you retired give me the job that  you almost came back out of retirement  for  first of all when I left Dallas you know  and I did Fox for a couple years  uh Philadelphia you know Jeffrey Louis  who's a good friend you know he talked  to me a couple different times uh and I  thought long and hard about it uh and  then uh McKay with Tampa I thought long  and hard about that one  uh Tom Benson came down Bob McNair came  down to Houston New Orleans and yeah I  thought about it Philadelphia one was  one that I kind of was interested any  college jobs but the bottom the bottom  line was I wanted to live here in South  Florida so that's why I ended up taking  the dolphin job  did you think about going back to the U  at all any any point oh oh no yeah I  mean I love University of Miami and  they're struggling right now but I think  they got the right head coach for Muriel  crispo I think they will be fine in time  but this is a struggle this year  it's amazing you've done all these  things but you're still identified as  the former Cowboys head coach it feels  like I mean that's the power of that  brand  um  do you give unvarnished opinions about  the Cowboys or is there question you  know sometimes the problem is anytime I  say anything about the Cowboys positive  or negative yeah that's going to be a  headline in Dallas and uh I mean you  know hey I'm not always right obviously  no one's always right uh but they're  going to ask my opinion and I'm going to  give it uh I mean I actually said A week  ago I thought this was the best cowboy  team that I've seen in a while now they  played poorly against Green Bay they  turned the ball over they didn't play  good run defense and that was a  disappointment but I think this is a  talented Cowboy statement in the NFC  you know there's four or five teams at  any one of the five could be in the  Super Bowl I don't think there's a great  team in the NFC yeah uh and so any one  of the five can make it there are a lot  of coaches former coaches uh of you know  front office people that were deeply  offended that Jeff Saturday moved in  front of the line for the Colts you as a  former coach and now analyst your  reaction  I don't know that I was offended I  understand it  and professional football only one vote  counts the guy that owns the team and he  can hire whoever he wants to hire uh and  I don't think we should be surprised by  any of them hiring because some of those  guys that owned the team you know maybe  they don't make good decisions now Jeff  Saturday a really a good guy he's been  around football a long time even though  he hasn't coached other than High School  uh I would say that you know there's  hundreds of  black and white coaches out there  they're a lot more qualified uh but I  wasn't offended  yeah I you know people jumped on it and  then he ends up winning that game and  then I wondered you know sometimes as a  head coach you have to let others do  their jobs when when you get to Sunday  just do your job maybe you don't coach  as much on Sunday as you do during the  week does that make sense well the one  thing Jeff Setter did he put uh Matt  Ryan back in there quarterback  I mean I mean so that he made one good  decision there  and and you know being the head coach uh  I think it's uh  the longevity is what you've got to look  at I mean it's a very difficult job  you've got to deal with a lot of  different people from diverse you know  backgrounds you got to deal with those  assistant coaches you got to deal with  you know 50 or 60 players uh so there's  a lot more to it than just coaching one  game is there a Michael Irvin's story in  the book  oh I'm sure Michael's in there somewhere  we've got Michael Urban stories we've  got Alonzo has Miss North hey one time  before we played Oklahoma we got some  stories about them calling Brian  Bosworth and then we got world wide west  and Leon Rose they drove my Corvette  from uh the NFC Championship Game to  Atlanta before the Super Bowl uh we've  got a lot of good stories in there  what's the craziest story though that  the ever encountered  the craziest story well actually the the  craziest story of uh when I was at  University of Miami is when Melvin  Breton and Alonso and and uh Jerome  Brown them called Brian Bosworth before  we play the morning before pregame meal  and said hey guys you awake you awake  well we're fixing to kick your ass  and then they come running to me in the  pre-game meals and Coach guess what we  did  what am I going to say I'm not going to  jump onto them  and which they they did but that was  part of swagger you know Michael Irvin  said you know hey you know part of  swagger and he said what we did he said  we're gonna you know we're gonna tell  you we're gonna kick your rear end then  we're gonna kick your in and then later  on we're going to talk about kicking  your rear end but he didn't say rear end  yeah I'm sure but coaching Somebody Like  Mike who you know had great Passion but  you always probably knew that you could  get a phone call at night that Mike  might be uh in trouble  Michael Who Never Was a problem now I  think after I left you know he got off  the rails just a little bit uh but it  was funny you know like if I hollered it  Emmett Emmett would go into a shell I  wouldn't get anything out of him the  rest of practice if I hollered it  Michael I could cuss him like a sailor  he had just worked that much harder you  know I hollered at Charles Haley a few  times Charles came my office and he said  coach I love playing for you just don't  embarrass me in front of the other  players so you had to know how to deal  with each individual how about Troy  I still love that story and I'm  paraphrasing but when you said that if  somebody fell asleep in a meeting I'd  cut him but yeah but if Troy fell asleep  in a meeting  I tell him to wake up  I told every team I said listen I'm  going to be very consistent I'm going to  treat every single one of you  differently  and and I said how I treat you the  harder you work the more you meet the  rules and regulation the more you do  what I ask you to do and the better  player you are the higher you are up on  the scale if you don't do those things  you're not a good player you got no  margin of error it's great to talk to  you the book is uh Swagger Super Bowls  brass balls and footballs we're talking  to Jimmy Johnson and get your copy  online wherever books are sold you look  great you sound great uh and uh  hopefully you do well with the book  Thank you again all right Dan hey enjoy  talking with you that's uh Jimmy Johnson  Hall of Famer take a break get phone  calls coming up Mike shazefski coming up  next hour

